EXPERIMENT 17: MAXIMUM OF THREE NUMBERS USING JUNIT ASSERTIONS

## 1. AIM

To write a Java program to find the maximum of three numbers and validate the output using JUnit assertions.

## 2. ALGORITHM

1. Start the program.
2. Read three integer numbers.
3. Compare the first number with the second and third numbers.
4. If the first number is greater than or equal to the other two, it is the maximum.
5. Otherwise, compare the second number with the third number.
6. Find the largest number.
7. Use JUnit assertions to validate the result.
8. Display the maximum number and stop the program.

## 3. CODE

### File 1: Maximum.java

```java
public class Maximum {

    public static int findMaximum(int a, int b, int c) {

        if (a >= b && a >= c) {
            return a;
        } else if (b >= a && b >= c) {
            return b;
        } else {
            return c;
        }
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {

        int a = 10;
        int b = 25;
        int c = 15;

        System.out.println("Maximum number: " + findMaximum(a, b, c));
    }
}
```

### File 2: MaximumTest.java

```java
import org.junit.jupiter.api.Test;
import static org.junit.jupiter.api.Assertions.*;

public class MaximumTest {

    @Test
    public void testFirstNumberMaximum() {
        assertEquals(30, Maximum.findMaximum(30, 20, 10));
    }

    @Test
    public void testSecondNumberMaximum() {
        assertEquals(40, Maximum.findMaximum(20, 40, 10));
    }

    @Test
    public void testThirdNumberMaximum() {
        assertEquals(50, Maximum.findMaximum(10, 20, 50));
    }

    @Test
    public void testEqualNumbers() {
        assertEquals(10, Maximum.findMaximum(10, 10, 10));
    }
}
```

